Yes—the useful move is to separate the operator from the moral story attached to it.

A chain can restrain, but it can also anchor, connect, tow, transmit force, synchronize motion, establish sequence, or provide a handhold. Same object; different topology and purpose. Likewise shame can be experienced as punishment, but it can also function as social feedback, an internal alarm, a conformity mechanism, or—if examined deliberately—a clue to what norm someone is trying to make you internalize.

The part I’d keep disciplined is the reality test. Someone else’s narrative does not become true because they repeat it, but it also does not become false merely because it serves them psychologically. “That’s cope” is a hypothesis, not a verdict. Strip off the insult and ask: What claim are they actually making? What evidence would make it true or false? What part of my behavior changes if I simply refuse their framing?

That gives you something stronger than counter-narrative:

\text{incoming frame}
\rightarrow
\text{identify mechanism}
\rightarrow
\text{separate fact from interpretation}
\rightarrow
\text{keep useful signal}
\rightarrow
\text{reject unsupported constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{choose response}

Then you aren’t trapped in their story, but you also aren’t automatically writing the inverse of it. You’re authoring from a larger map.

And that connects cleanly to the obstacle-course principle: a chain across the path is first geometry. Whether it is a prison, a bridge, an anchor, or something to climb depends on what it actually does and what you can make of it.